 ">     ],",
 ">     \"synthesis\": \"Section 1.3 supplies the **integral** half of vector calculus, to pair with the differential operators gradient, divergence, and curl built earlier in the chapter.\\\\n\\\\n**1.3.1** defines the three species of integral. Line integrals $\\\\\\\\int \\\\\\\\mathbf{v}\\\\\\\\cdot d\\\\\\\\mathbf{l}$ sum the component of a vector along a prescribed path; surface integrals $\\\\\\\\int \\\\\\\\mathbf{v}\\\\\\\\cdot d\\\\\\\\mathbf{a}$ (flux) sum the normal component through a surface; volume integrals $\\\\\\\\int T\\\\\\\\,d\\\\\\\\tau$ sum a scalar over a region. Example 1.6 establishes the crucial *negative* result: the same vector can give 11 along one path and 10 along another to the same endpoint, so line integrals are ordinarily **path dependent**. The section flags repeatedly that a special class of integrands escapes this dependence, and that characterizing it is the work of the fundamental theorems.\\\\n\\\\n**1.3.2-1.3.3** establish the template and its first instance. The fundamental theorem of calculus, $\\\\\\\\int_a^b (df/dx)dx = f(b) - f(a)$, says the integral of a derivative over a region equals the value of the function at the **boundary** of that region. Chopping the interval into pieces and adding the increments $df$ reproduces the endpoint difference. Carrying the same reasoning into three dimensions — summing $dT = (\\\\\\\\nabla T)\\\\\\\\cdot d\\\\\\\\mathbf{l}$ along a path — gives the fundamental theorem for gradients, $\\\\\\\\int_{\\\\\\\\mathbf{a}}^{\\\\\\\\mathbf{b}}(\\\\\\\\nabla T)\\\\\\\\cdot d\\\\\\\\mathbf{l} = T(\\\\\\\\mathbf{b}) - T(\\\\\\\\mathbf{a})$, whose right side makes no reference to the path. Two corollaries follow immediately: gradient line integrals are path independent, and the closed loop integral vanishes. Example 1.9 confirms both, giving 2 on a two-leg path and 2 on the straight line.\\\\n\\\\n**1.3.4** applies the template to the divergence. The divergence theorem, $\\\\\\\\int_{\\\\\\\\mathcal{V}}(\\\\\\\\nabla\\\\\\\\cdot\\\\\\\\mathbf{v})d\\\\\\\\tau = \\\\\\\\oint_{\\\\\\\\mathcal{S}}\\\\\\\\mathbf{v}\\\\\\\\cdot d\\\\\\\\mathbf{a}$, relates the volume integral of the divergence to the flux through the bounding surface. The boundary term is now itself an integral, because the boundary of a volume is a closed surface rather than a pair of points. The faucet picture — counting sources inside versus measuring flow out through the boundary — makes it plausible, and Example 1.10 verifies both sides equal 2 on a unit cube.\\\\n\\\\n**1.3.5** applies the template to the curl. Stokes' theorem, $\\\\\\\\int_{\\\\\\\\mathcal{S}}(\\\\\\\\nabla\\\\\\\\times\\\\\\\\mathbf{v})\\\\\\\\cdot d\\\\\\\\mathbf{a} = \\\\\\\\oint_{\\\\\\\\mathcal{P}}\\\\\\\\mathbf{v}\\\\\\\\cdot d\\\\\\\\mathbf{l}$, equates the total swirl over a patch to the circulation around its boundary. Where a surface integral would normally depend on the surface chosen, the curl integral does not, because the right side refers only to the boundary — the soap-film argument. The theorem's residual sign ambiguity (which way around the loop? which way does $d\\\\\\\\mathbf{a}$ point?) is resolved by the right-hand rule. Example 1.11 checks both sides at 4/3 and yields the section's most practical habit: always write $d\\\\\\\\mathbf{l} = dx\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\mathbf{x}} + dy\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\mathbf{y}} + dz\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\mathbf{z}}$ with no minus signs and let the limits carry the direction.\\\\n\\\\n**1.3.6** closes the loop by showing how to move derivatives between factors at all. Integration by parts, derived from the product rule plus the fundamental theorem, transfers a derivative from $g$ to $f$ at the cost of a minus sign and a boundary term; the vector version does the same for $\\\\\\\\nabla\\\\\\\\cdot(f\\\\\\\\mathbf{A})$, moving the derivative from $\\\\\\\\mathbf{A}$ to $f$ where it becomes a gradient.\\\\n\\\\nRunning through all six subsections is a single idea, stated explicitly on page 48: there are three species of derivative, each has its own fundamental theorem, and **every one of them has the same format — the integral of a derivative over a region is given by the value of the function at the boundary.**\"",
